# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
**/mutants.out*/
*.pdb

# ── NO ELIXIR / `server/` BLOCK, DELIBERATELY ───────────────────────────────
# This file carried seven Elixir ignore rules until 2026-09-27:
#
#   /server/_build/  /server/deps/  /server/*.ez  /server/cover/  /server/doc/
#   /.elixir_ls/     erl_crash.dump
#
# Issue #74 retired this repository's own Elixir relay in favour of the external
# metadatastician/burble game-session fabric (ADR-0006), and the `server/` tree
# went with it. `git ls-files` shows no `.ex`, `.exs`, `.beam`, or `mix.lock`
# tracked here, so every one of those patterns matched nothing.
#
# Dead ignore rules are not harmless. They tell a reader this repository
# contains an Elixir build tree that it does not, and — the part that actually
# cost something — the matching corpse in .github/dependabot.yml kept a weekly
# `hex in /server` update failing for a year (run 36117293992, 2026-09-25:
# "Dependabot encountered an error performing the update"). A permanently red
# run nobody reads trains everyone to ignore red runs.
#
# The pinned burble-client is consumed as a Cargo git dependency, so it is
# covered by /target/ and Cargo.lock like every other Rust dependency. If an
# Elixir tree returns, restore the rules AND the dependabot ecosystem together:
# tests/ci_security_config_test.sh now fails any ecosystem whose directory does
# not exist, which is the check that would have caught this in the week it
# happened rather than a year later.

# Local tool state and editors
/.agents/
/.codex/
/.claude/
/.idea/
/.vscode/
/.mise.local.toml
/.env
/.env.*
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Local outputs
/logs/
/scripts/tmp/
*.log
*.tmp

# Idris2 build outputs (crates/idaptik-ffi/abi and its smoke)
crates/idaptik-ffi/abi/build/
crates/idaptik-ffi/abi/smoke/build/
.zig-cache/
.probe
.double-free-probe

# why3find local prover cache (per-machine; proof sessions live in verif/)
.why3find/

# ── GAME AUTHORING AND ASSET PIPELINE ───────────────────────────────────────
# `source/` is the planned home of authored content (CC-BY-SA-4.0), tracked in
# full. `assets/` will hold the Bevy-loadable artefacts generated from it and is
# tracked: the engine loads them at runtime and a checkout that cannot run the
# game is not a checkout of the game. What is ignored here is the intermediate
# machinery between the two — caches, scratch renders, and editor state — which
# is reproducible, per-machine, and enormous.
#
# Generated asset outputs land in assets/, never in source/. Anything matching
# the patterns below is pipeline scratch and must not be committed. The patterns
# are declared before the trees exist on purpose: an ignore rule added at the
# same moment as the first artefact arrives is an ignore rule added after that
# artefact has already been committed, and un-committing a large binary from
# history is a rewrite, not an edit.

# Importer and DCC scratch state. Each of these is written by a tool named in
# source/art/PIPELINE.md or source/audio/PIPELINE.md; none is an input.
/.import/
/.godot/
/assets/.cache/
/assets/**/.import/
**/*.import

# Blender and image-editor autosave / backup files.
**/*.blend[0-9]
**/*.blend1
**/*~.blend
**/*.psd.tmp
**/*.aseprite-wip

# Audio editor and DAW scratch.
**/*.aup3-wal
**/*.aup3-shm
**/*.peak
**/*.reapeaks
**/*.sfk

# Video editor render scratch and intermediate proxies. Proxies are regenerated
# from the authored master, which is what source/video/ holds.
/proxies/
**/*.proxy.*
**/*.prmd
**/*.mex

# Shader and texture compilation caches.
/.shader-cache/
/.naga-cache/
**/*.spv.tmp

# Bevy runtime scratch written next to the executable during a dev session.
/saves/
/screenshots/
/recordings/
*.sav

# ── LOCAL PLAYER DATA: NEVER TRACKED, NEVER SYNCED ──────────────────────────
# settings/bits are the player's own storage — progression, keybinds, audio
# levels, accessibility preferences. They are per-machine and per-player by
# definition, they must not feed the deterministic simulation (see
# source/settings/README.md), and committing one player's save into a shared
# repository is both a privacy failure and a determinism failure.
/local/
**/player-profile.json
**/local-settings.json
**/*.save.json

# ── GENERATED DOCUMENTATION OUTPUT ──────────────────────────────────────────
# docs/wiki/ is authored and tracked. The rendered static site is not.
/docs/_build/
/.berrywiki-cache/

# ── GITHUB PAGES BUILD SCRATCH ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# .github/workflows/pages.yml clones the Ddraig SSG into .ddraig-ssg/, runs
# `mkdir -p src`, copies README.md to src/index.md when src/ is absent, and
# builds to _site/. NONE of that is tracked: `src/` does not exist in this
# repository and is created fresh on the runner each build.
#
# Both were unignored until 2026-09-27. That is not a hypothetical risk -- it is
# a trap with a specific shape. Anyone reproducing the Pages build locally gets
# a working tree containing src/index.md (a copy of README.md) and a full
# _site/ tree, and `git add -A` commits both. The committed src/index.md then
# shadows the runner's README.md copy forever, so the deployed site quietly
# stops tracking the README, and _site/ adds a rendered duplicate of every page
# to the diff of every subsequent documentation change.
#
# NOTE the name collision this sits next to: `src/` here is Pages scratch, while
# the planned `source/` tree is tracked, authored game content under
# CC-BY-SA-4.0. They are different directories with different licence layers and
# different fates, and the similarity of their names is the reason both rules
# are written out in full here rather than left to be inferred.
/.ddraig-ssg/
/src/
/_site/
/site/

# Content-addressed gate ledgers (scripts/lib/content-cache.sh). Regenerable,
# local, never committed: a cache that survives a clone is a cache nobody can
# reproduce.
/.cache/
